WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.870 --> 00:00:03.420
- Welcome, welcome, welcome in.

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:06.780
Welcome to another
edition of "Market Takes."

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:08.190
I know you could have
been anywhere in the world

00:00:08.190 --> 00:00:10.320
but you're here with me
and I appreciate that.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:11.880
I'm your host, Dion Rabouin,

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:13.680
reporter for The Wall Street Journal.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:16.740
This is "Market Takes" and
this week we are taking you

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:19.380
through the three most
important things that you need

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:20.914
to look out for this week.

00:00:20.914 --> 00:00:22.140
We've got a lot of data coming up

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:25.200
as we prepare for our
last week before we get

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:28.410
into the Federal Reserve's
FOMC meeting next week.

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:30.540
So I'm gonna tell you what
the three biggest reports

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:31.620
are gonna be this week.

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:34.260
First off, we've got April Consumer Credit

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:35.910
coming out on Wednesday.

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:37.530
We get mortgage rates on Thursday

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:40.860
and then initial jobless claims
that same day on Thursday.

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:43.710
Now, those aren't generally
thought of as big reports

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:47.130
but ahead of the Fed, ahead
of CPI, ahead of retail sales

00:00:47.130 --> 00:00:48.540
all coming out next week.

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:50.130
I'm gonna tell you why
you need to watch out

00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:52.500
for all three of those reports

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:54.480
and the effect they
could have on inflation.

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:55.410
And as we know,

00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:57.660
that is all the Fed is
looking out for right now.

00:00:57.660 --> 00:00:58.950
So first off, let's talk

00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:01.080
through this April Consumer Credit Report.

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:03.120
Now, that usually gets
released or doesn't usually.

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:05.040
That gets released a
little later in the day.

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:07.680
It's at 3 PM on Wednesday.

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:11.400
March, we had a jump of $26.5 billion

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:13.710
and for April, we're
expecting another jump

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:17.940
of $21.6 billion on the increase.

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:21.090
Really, without context, that
doesn't mean a whole lot,

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:22.200
so let me throw a chart up here

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:24.660
on your screen so you can
just see what we're looking at

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:27.120
when it comes to credit card increases.

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:29.280
Now, as you see up there on your screen,

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:32.700
just a big increase and
you see that spiking number

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:36.480
as we get through 2021,
2022 and then 2023.

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:41.340
So 2023 has even risen
faster than previous years

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:43.980
and there is a particular data
point I wanna talk through

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:45.870
but just looking up here on your screen,

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:47.880
you can see that big defined pickup

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:51.300
that really outstrips
the previous increases

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:52.770
that we had seen in other years.

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:55.620
You've just seen that spike
being a little bit more vertical

00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:56.453
than it has been

00:01:56.453 --> 00:01:58.740
and that chart just
illustrates what we have seen

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:01.620
in terms of Americans
taking on credit card debt.

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:04.230
Now, a very interesting fact
that I haven't seen talked

00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:06.000
about very much is for the first time

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:08.940
in 20 years during the
first quarter of 2023,

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:11.640
Americans did not reduce
credit card spending.

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:14.610
We have seen a consistent theme
of Americans build up a lot

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:16.860
of credit card spending
in the fourth quarter.

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:20.220
Obviously, that's holiday
time, gift giving, flying back

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:22.650
and forth, travel to see
family, et cetera, et cetera.

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:24.150
But then in the first quarter,

00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:26.340
consistently over the past 20 years,

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:28.050
Americans reduce that credit card debt.

00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:30.480
They pay it down in the
beginning of the year.

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:31.500
We did not see that.

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:32.878
And in fact, we saw Americans taking

00:02:32.878 --> 00:02:36.060
on more credit card debt in
the first quarter of this year.

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:37.890
And that theme looks to have continued

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:39.240
into the second quarter.

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:40.500
Now usually, like I said,

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:43.170
you get your tax refund,
you pay that down.

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:44.430
So the things, begin,

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:46.650
beginning of the year, tax refund season,

00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:49.350
those things usually go hand in hand

00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:51.240
with a reduction in credit card debt.

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:52.770
This year, we have seen an increase

00:02:52.770 --> 00:02:54.960
in credit card debt and
actually, an acceleration

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:56.940
of the increase in credit card debt.

00:02:56.940 --> 00:02:58.380
This is suggesting that some households

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:01.140
are under strained from
these higher prices,

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:03.630
higher inflation and are likely relying

00:03:03.630 --> 00:03:06.150
on credit cards to
maintain their spending.

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:08.580
So we have seen retail sales last month.

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:09.413
We saw pick up.

00:03:09.413 --> 00:03:12.540
We've seen spending
especially on services pick up

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:14.040
and a lot of folks are saying, "Okay,

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:16.080
well, that means the
economy is healthy combined

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:17.880
with the jobs numbers that we've seen."

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:19.476
But what it likely means is a little bit

00:03:19.476 --> 00:03:21.990
of that psychological effect

00:03:21.990 --> 00:03:23.538
of folks are saying,
"Okay, well, I got a job.

00:03:23.538 --> 00:03:24.990
Job may not pay enough

00:03:24.990 --> 00:03:27.060
for me to afford all the
things that I want to,

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:29.280
buy the things that I
need, but I've got the job,

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:31.860
so let me go ahead and take
out money on this credit card.

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:34.080
I'll buy things on my credit
card, pay it off later."

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:35.760
Well, those balances
are just getting higher

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:37.260
and higher and higher.

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:38.550
Interesting fact, follow with me.

00:03:38.550 --> 00:03:39.720
I'm gonna throw a bunch
of numbers out here

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:41.160
but follow along with me.

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:44.222
2018, credit card debt rose 4.2%.

00:03:44.222 --> 00:03:47.910
2019, credit card debt rose
3.5% again year over year.

00:03:47.910 --> 00:03:48.743
Right?

00:03:48.743 --> 00:03:53.250
2020, pandemic year, credit
card debt declined by 10.9%.

00:03:53.250 --> 00:03:56.670
Then 2021, credit card debt rose by 5.7%.

00:03:56.670 --> 00:04:00.900
2022, credit card debt rose by 17.4%

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:03.780
and we are on pace in 2023.

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:06.060
In March, we had 17.9%.

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:08.190
In April, we had 13.6%.

00:04:08.190 --> 00:04:09.600
Now, look at this chart again.

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:11.970
Let's throw this chart up here
on your screen one more time

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:15.330
and you see that define
that very clear acceleration

00:04:15.330 --> 00:04:16.950
in credit card debt

00:04:16.950 --> 00:04:19.110
and we are just seeing
a huge massive pickup

00:04:19.110 --> 00:04:21.120
and that's showing no signs of falling.

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:23.100
It is reversing

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:26.670
and defying historical norms
and just really picking up.

00:04:26.670 --> 00:04:28.350
So that chart illustrating again

00:04:28.350 --> 00:04:31.290
just this unusual change that we have seen

00:04:31.290 --> 00:04:33.120
in credit card borrowing
that I think is going

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:35.880
along with the increase in jobs numbers

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:36.713
that we have seen.

00:04:36.713 --> 00:04:37.546
That is unusual.

00:04:37.546 --> 00:04:39.300
Usually as folks get
jobs, they got more money,

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:40.860
they're paying down credit card debt.

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:42.690
What we were seeing
instead is people taking

00:04:42.690 --> 00:04:44.520
on more credit card debt.

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:46.680
We're also seeing
delinquencies rising back

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:47.820
to pre-pandemic levels.

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:49.694
So folks not being able to pay off

00:04:49.694 --> 00:04:51.570
that credit card debt they took on.

00:04:51.570 --> 00:04:53.700
And we know that credit
card interest rates

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:56.610
are at a record high, well above 20%,

00:04:56.610 --> 00:04:58.710
by some measures, up to 24%.

00:04:58.710 --> 00:05:00.960
So this is again, defying historical norms

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:02.940
and at a time when credit card debt

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:05.250
is the most expensive it's ever been.

00:05:05.250 --> 00:05:07.860
Now, look, wanna talk about
another piece that plays

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:09.930
into that debt puzzle and
that is mortgage rates.

00:05:09.930 --> 00:05:11.010
Now, take a look here

00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:13.680
at the Freddie Mac mortgage rates data.

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:15.120
Now, we are seeing those mortgage rates

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:17.476
again approaching 7%.

00:05:17.476 --> 00:05:20.280
When mortgage rates first
started picking up in 2022,

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:23.520
you saw home sales decline,
really dive, fall off a cliff.

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:25.140
But lately, we have not seen that,

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:27.540
even though we're seeing this up and down

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:31.320
in mortgage rates increasing, decreasing.

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:33.660
But as the 10-year
Treasury yield has risen,

00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:35.820
it's around 3.75% right now.

00:05:35.820 --> 00:05:38.340
You see there at the edge of
your screen on the right side,

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:40.140
mortgage rates starting to pick back up.

00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:42.720
Now, that chart just
illustrates the overall trend

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:46.200
of mortgage rates and what
we are seeing is housing,

00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:48.810
buyers of new homes and
existing homes starting

00:05:48.810 --> 00:05:52.050
to pick back up again defying
even these higher rates.

00:05:52.050 --> 00:05:54.630
So even as rates have come down

00:05:54.630 --> 00:05:56.010
and picked back up and sort of stayed

00:05:56.010 --> 00:05:57.990
around the same level
they were at last year,

00:05:57.990 --> 00:06:01.500
we are not seeing consumers
turning down borrowing.

00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:03.390
And actually, the Fed is
starting to worry about this.

00:06:03.390 --> 00:06:05.400
We've seen Fed governor Michelle Bowman

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:07.327
just last week say, quote,

00:06:07.327 --> 00:06:10.260
"While we expect lower rents
will eventually be reflected

00:06:10.260 --> 00:06:11.093
in inflation data

00:06:11.093 --> 00:06:13.440
as new leases make their
way into calculations,

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:17.445
the residential real estate
market appears to be rebounding

00:06:17.445 --> 00:06:19.650
with home prices leveling out recently,

00:06:19.650 --> 00:06:23.010
which has implications for
our fight to lower inflation."

00:06:23.010 --> 00:06:25.087
That's Fed Governor Michelle
Bowman talking about,

00:06:25.087 --> 00:06:26.880
"Hey, we think this could play

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:28.860
into our fight against inflation.

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:30.870
We wanna lower inflation,
we're keeping rates high.

00:06:30.870 --> 00:06:33.060
That's choking off growth for businesses

00:06:33.060 --> 00:06:35.760
but folks are taking out that
debt and on the consumer side

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:37.800
and credit cards and
in terms of mortgages."

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:40.350
So that chart is, like I said,

00:06:40.350 --> 00:06:42.390
illustrating that folks
are not turning away

00:06:42.390 --> 00:06:45.120
from mortgages even as
these rates move higher.

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:46.771
Last chart I want to talk to you about

00:06:46.771 --> 00:06:50.160
is the initial jobless
claims and what we're seeing,

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:54.000
we saw this surprisingly strong
job report data on Friday

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:57.000
and we have seen initial jobless
claims really surprise us.

00:06:57.000 --> 00:06:58.080
Wanna put up here a chart

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:00.810
on your screen now that
shows initial jobless claims.

00:07:00.810 --> 00:07:03.480
This is not seasonally
adjusted right here.

00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:05.100
You see that big spike at
the beginning of the year

00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:07.740
and we've seen the numbers
kind of pick up and fall down

00:07:07.740 --> 00:07:09.930
but you're seeing big spikes

00:07:09.930 --> 00:07:13.020
in pickup when the data is not
seasonally adjusted, right?

00:07:13.020 --> 00:07:15.390
You see a boom, pickup, oh,
okay, that could be worrisome.

00:07:15.390 --> 00:07:17.070
Now I wanna put up here on your screen

00:07:17.070 --> 00:07:20.640
the seasonally adjusted when
smoothing out that data.

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:22.530
Well, it looks a lot less worrisome.

00:07:22.530 --> 00:07:24.960
You're seeing a lot lower numbers

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:28.680
and consistently, the trend
is much more consistently low

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:30.150
than it has been historically.

00:07:30.150 --> 00:07:33.150
And again, the Fed wants
to see more jobless claims,

00:07:33.150 --> 00:07:34.620
the Fed wants to see more folks filing

00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:36.000
those unemployment claims

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:38.220
because that's gonna
slow the labor market,

00:07:38.220 --> 00:07:39.840
that's gonna slow down the economy.

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:41.610
And that chart is illustrating

00:07:41.610 --> 00:07:45.000
that we have not seen, once
the data has been smoothed out,

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:47.040
a big pickup in initial jobless claims,

00:07:47.040 --> 00:07:49.770
a big pickup in folks
filing for unemployment.

00:07:49.770 --> 00:07:51.510
And another big increase

00:07:51.510 --> 00:07:53.010
that we got, or another change we got

00:07:53.010 --> 00:07:56.280
that was very meaningful was
Massachusetts removed a number

00:07:56.280 --> 00:07:58.980
of fraudulent claims from
its unemployment data

00:07:58.980 --> 00:08:03.030
and that led us to the biggest
initial jobless claims drop

00:08:03.030 --> 00:08:04.923
that we had seen since 2021.

00:08:06.090 --> 00:08:06.937
Massachusetts said, quote,

00:08:06.937 --> 00:08:08.490
"It was experiencing an increase

00:08:08.490 --> 00:08:10.320
in fraudulent claim activities

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:12.420
and unadjusted claims
fell by more than 2,000."

00:08:12.420 --> 00:08:13.680
That's big for one state

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:16.050
in a state that's really
not all that large.

00:08:16.050 --> 00:08:19.530
So labor market has only
slowed down a little bit

00:08:19.530 --> 00:08:23.010
even with 500 basis points
of Fed rate increases.

00:08:23.010 --> 00:08:24.930
That's not a good sign for the Fed

00:08:24.930 --> 00:08:26.880
if what they wanna do is
slow down labor market,

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:29.670
which we know they do
because they've said as much.

00:08:29.670 --> 00:08:32.940
Fewer layoffs mean the Fed
rate hikes aren't working

00:08:32.940 --> 00:08:34.890
and the labor market is not weakening,

00:08:34.890 --> 00:08:36.480
which means, again, as we've seen,

00:08:36.480 --> 00:08:38.280
folks are spending on those credit cards

00:08:38.280 --> 00:08:39.810
and they're buying more things

00:08:39.810 --> 00:08:42.240
and the inflation rate is not coming down

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:43.350
the way they want it to.

00:08:43.350 --> 00:08:45.180
Listen, this all comes together

00:08:45.180 --> 00:08:47.310
in that consumers have more jobs

00:08:47.310 --> 00:08:49.650
so they feel more
confident in taking on debt

00:08:49.650 --> 00:08:50.970
even if they can't buy things now.

00:08:50.970 --> 00:08:52.530
They say, "Hey, we'll put
it on the credit card."

00:08:52.530 --> 00:08:54.570
We're seeing declining loans from banks.

00:08:54.570 --> 00:08:56.400
We're seeing lower personal
loans and lower loans

00:08:56.400 --> 00:08:58.800
to businesses and we know
the economy is slowing

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:00.180
but consumers are still spending.

00:09:00.180 --> 00:09:01.770
That's keeping inflation high.

00:09:01.770 --> 00:09:04.410
Fed has a big decision to make this week.

00:09:04.410 --> 00:09:06.570
The market is largely expecting
that they're gonna hold.

00:09:06.570 --> 00:09:09.270
We will see what happens
at the Fed Rate Conference

00:09:09.270 --> 00:09:11.130
and listen, that's gonna do it for us here

00:09:11.130 --> 00:09:12.120
on "Market Takes."

00:09:12.120 --> 00:09:13.500
I know you could have
been anywhere in the world

00:09:13.500 --> 00:09:15.780
but you were here with
me and I appreciate that.

00:09:15.780 --> 00:09:17.820
I'm Dion Rabouin for
The Wall Street Journal.

00:09:17.820 --> 00:09:19.443
We will see you next time.

